# A verb such as `ensure` or `means` takes either a noun (`ensure the
# order`) or a clause, a subject and its verb (`ensure the order
# holds`). Without `that`, the reader takes the noun as the verb's
# object and has to reread when the clause's own verb arrives. `that`
# marks the clause from its first word (`ensure that the order holds`).
#
# The rule counts the verb when a determiner (a word that introduces a
# noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`), one to three more words, and
# then an auxiliary (a helping verb such as `is`, `has`, or `can`) or
# one of `exists`, `runs`, `matches`, `contains`, and `fails` follow it.
# The words in between exclude `that`, `which`, `who`, `to`, pronouns,
# conjunctions, and commas, because each one ends the noun or opens a
# clause of its own (`check your docs to be sure`). The filter narrows
# the finding to the verb. `specify`, `see`, `states`, and `find` stay
# out, because a noun and a description of it follow them more often
# than a clause does. `this` and `which` before a verb that ends in `s`
# skip the match, because the broad-reference rule reports `this means`
# and `, which means`.
#
# It hit 82 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled hits
# dropped the `that` before a clause.
expr imper [ensure, verify, confirm, (make sure), assume, check, remember, notice]
expr third [ensures, means, says, knows, shows, indicates, guarantees, expects, tells]
expr aux [AUX, exists, runs, matches, contains, fails]
expr w ![that, which, who, to, PRON, CCONJ, (,)]
expr det <[DET, your, its, their, our], !that>
expr rest @det [(@w @aux), (@w @w @aux), (@w @w @w @aux)]
expr main [<(@imper @rest), @imper>, <(![this, which] @third @rest), @third>]
let message "Missing that. Write that after the verb, so the noun after it does not read as its object."
let description "Flags ensure, means, and similar verbs that open a clause without that."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Ensure that the file exists."
allows "Check the file before the build."
allows "This means the pool is empty."
allows "Check your docs to be sure."
